@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
title Review gate transport — why it is native-Linux only, and how to cross platforms

node "Native Linux — WORKS" as LINUX <<host>> {
  component "proveo\ngate listens on review.sock" as PL <<app>>
  component "inspector sidecar\nconnect() succeeds" as IL <<app>>
}

node "macOS / Windows — FAILS CLOSED" as VM <<error>> {
  component "proveo (host OS)\nsocket created here" as PM <<app>>
  component "virtiofs · osxfs · 9p\nfilesystem virtualization" as FS <<cloud>>
  component "inspector (Linux VM)\nsees the inode, connect() fails" as IM <<error>>
}

PL ARROW_MAIN IL : same kernel, same socket object
PM ARROW_CLOUD FS : bind mount
FS ARROW_ERROR IM : inode projected, AF_UNIX not carried

note bottom of LINUX
  **Why it works here.** A bind mount on native Linux is the same kernel's
  mount namespace pointing at the same inode. An ""AF_UNIX"" socket IS that
  inode plus a kernel socket object, and both sides share the kernel — so
  ""connect()"" reaches the listener proveo created.

  Necessary but NOT sufficient: a remote ""DOCKER_HOST"" puts the mount on
  another machine, where the socket does not exist at all. The gate requires
  Linux **and** a local daemon.
end note

note bottom of VM
  **Why it cannot work here.** The container runs inside a Linux VM and the
  bind mount crosses a filesystem-virtualization layer. That layer projects
  the socket's directory entry — ""ls"" shows it, ""stat"" reports a socket — but
  it does not proxy ""AF_UNIX"" semantics. There is no shared kernel socket
  object, so ""connect()"" fails.

  The failure mode is the dangerous part: ""AskOverSocket"" cannot reach the
  gate, fails closed, and every connection is denied **without a prompt**.
  A security gate that denies everything looks like it is working perfectly.
end note

note as Strategies #F4F9FB
  **Cross-platform strategies, most to least promising.**

  **A — file handshake over the mount already there.** Regular file I/O
  crosses every virtualization layer correctly; only ""AF_UNIX"" does not. The
  inspector writes ""pending/<id>.json"" and polls ""decisions/<id>.json"";
  proveo does the inverse. Reuses the existing bind mount, adds no network
  surface, and needs no new capability. Costs a poll loop — ""inotify"" does not
  propagate over virtiofs/osxfs, so watching is out and a ~100ms tick is
  required. Trivial against a 20s hold. **Simplest correct option.**

  **B — published port, inverted protocol.** ""-p 127.0.0.1:0:<port>"" on the
  inspector, with proveo as the CLIENT: blocking ""GET /pending"", then
  ""POST /decide"", plus a per-session token so a loopback port is not an open
  door. Port publishing behaves identically on macOS, Linux, Windows and
  Podman, and the sidecar still cannot initiate toward the host. Lower latency
  than A; costs a protocol inversion and an HTTP surface inside the inspector.

  **C — ""host.docker.internal"" / ""--add-host=host-gateway"". REJECTED.** It
  requires the inspector to route to the host gateway, but the inspector sits
  only on ""--internal"" networks and egresses solely through Squid. A sidecar
  that can reach the gateway can bypass Squid. This trades the egress boundary
  for a consent prompt, which is the wrong trade at any latency.

  **D — ""docker exec"" as the channel. REJECTED.** proveo already shells out to
  docker, but the egress-proxy image is distroless with no shell, and exec
  per decision is slow and racy.

  **E — attach the sidecar's stdio. REJECTED.** Would mean running the
  inspector in the foreground, conflicting with the detached-sidecar lifecycle
  the egress plan is built on — and proveo's own stdio is already committed to
  the agent's PTY.

  A and B are both genuinely portable. **A is preferred**: it changes the
  fewest moving parts, adds no listening socket anywhere, and the mount it
  needs is the one the gate already has.
end note

note bottom of IM
  **Detect, never assume.** ""reviewSupported"" gates on ""runtime.GOOS"" plus a
  local ""unix://"" daemon and greys the prompt option with the specific reason.
  An explicitly requested ""--egress-mode review"" on an unsupported host warns
  that connections will be denied. Until a portable transport lands, the tier
  must announce its own limits rather than appear strict.
end note
note bottom of FS
  **The socket path has a hard ceiling, and it differs per platform.**
  ""sockaddr_un.sun_path"" is 104 bytes on darwin/BSD and 108 on Linux, and
  ""bind"" fails outright past it — not truncates. A session state dir nested
  deep enough silently becomes unbindable, so past the limit the gate falls
  back to a short, collision-resistant name.

  **The fallback gets its own directory, never a bare file in TempDir.**
  The caller bind-mounts ""filepath\.Dir(socket)"" into the inspector, so a
  loose file in a shared temp directory would mount the whole of it.
end note


@enduml
